---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Brief = `For a class icebreaker I want people to type their birth
month as a number and get back which season they were born
in. Nothing fancy. It just has to be right.`;

const SampleOutput = `Birth month (1-13): 11

November is in Autumn.`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-1/cc-set-2" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back to Set 2
        </a>

        <h1>Code Challenge: Birth Seasons</h1>

        <h4>
          Difficulty&nbsp;&nbsp;
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="brief">
          <h2>The Brief</h2>

          <Code
            code={Brief}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="What they asked for"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b>Write your requirements list before you write any code.</b> Input,
            calculate, output, and anything you had to decide for yourself. See{" "}
            <a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-1/cc-set-2">Set 2</a> for what that looks like.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="notes">
          <h2>Notes</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>Months are numbers 1 through 12, and the seasons do not divide into neat groups of three at the start of the year.</li>
            <li>December, January, and February are the same season but they are not consecutive numbers. Think about how you group them.</li>
            <li>A month outside 1 to 12 should not silently fall into a season.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="where-to-start">
          <h2>Where to Start</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Only one-star challenges have this section.</b> If you already know how to
            begin, skip it &mdash; and when you move up to two stars, working out the order
            yourself is part of the job.
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li>Get the month and cast it to a number.</li>
            <li>Do spring, summer, and autumn first. They are three consecutive months each.</li>
            <li>Winter is last on purpose. December, January, and February are not consecutive numbers, so it needs a condition with an <code>||</code> in it.</li>
            <li>Finally, reject anything outside 1 to 12.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="sample-output">
          <h2>Sample Output</h2>

          <Code
            code={SampleOutput}
            frame="terminal"
            title="Sample Output"
            lang="txt"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-3">Test 12, 1, and 2. If any of them lands in the wrong season, your grouping is the problem.</p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Your requirements list and your program file, named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">cc-birth-seasons</b>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Challenge
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
